A second-cycle course (3MB00A, 7.5 ECTS) on the mathematical and statistical methods used in modern bioinformatics. Each topic pairs a method with a biological problem that motivates it: students never learn a technique in the abstract.
See audience.md (on GitHub) for the assumed background and courseplan.md for the formal syllabus.


By the end of the course, a student should be able to take a quantitative bioinformatics problem — solving a linear system, reducing the dimensionality of an expression matrix, modelling counts with the right distribution, or reasoning about a process as a Markov chain — and set it up, solve it in R, and judge whether the model is valid for the biology at hand.



The official syllabus groups outcomes into three categories.


A student can:

explain where common statistical distributions come from, and where they arise in biological data;
contrast Bayesian and frequentist approaches;
describe the solution space of a linear system, including over- and underdetermined systems (unique, none, infinitely many);
explain memoryless (Markov) processes;
distinguish linear from nonlinear systems;
explain what a latent space is and why dimensional reduction is useful.




A student can:

solve systems of linear equations (by hand for small systems, in R for larger ones);
formulate a biological problem as a Markov chain and analyse its behaviour;
analyse large-scale data in latent spaces, with linear (PCA / OLS) and nonlinear methods;
build hierarchical models with alternative distributions, in particular Poisson and negative binomial;
apply chemometrics and single-cell methods to large datasets.




A student can:

assess the validity of a mathematical model for a biological system — state its assumptions and recognise when they break.





Per the syllabus: an individual written on-campus exam. Pass (G) requires passing the exam; distinction (VG) requires distinction-level performance. Up to 10 % bonus may be awarded for participation. The self-paced exercises in this material exist to build the by-hand and by-code fluency the exam tests.

