Background
A cell is a point in gene-expression space (one axis per gene). Cells of the same type express similar genes, so they sit close together; the whole task is to find those close-together groups without being told the labels (unsupervised).
The standard pipeline, from the Dimensionality reduction & clustering lecture:
\[
\underbrace{X}_{\text{cells × genes}}
\xrightarrow{\text{PCA}}
\underbrace{Z}_{\text{cells × }\sim 30}
\xrightarrow{\text{kNN}}
\underbrace{G}_{\text{graph}}
\xrightarrow{\begin{array}{c}\text{UMAP}\\\text{clustering}\end{array}}
\text{2D map + labels}
\]

PCA compresses ~2000 genes to ~30 principal components — this denoises and speeds everything up. Distances are more meaningful in ~30 clean dimensions than in 2000 noisy ones. (Same method as Principal component analysis and PCA in R.)
kNN graph: for each cell, connect it to its \(k\) nearest neighbours (typical \(k \approx 15\)) by Euclidean distance in PC space, \[
\text{dist}(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{x}_j) = \lVert \mathbf{x}_i - \mathbf{x}_j \rVert = \sqrt{\sum_{p} (x_{ip} - x_{jp})^2}.
\]
UMAP lays the graph out in 2D. It is non-linear and preserves local neighbourhoods only — its axes and inter-cluster distances are not quantitative.
Clustering (Louvain / Leiden) cuts the same graph into densely connected communities; the resolution parameter controls how many. UMAP and clustering are two views of one graph, not one derived from the other.

Key discipline: local structure is trustworthy, global geometry is decorative.

A tiny concrete 2-state HMM
States \(\{H, L\}\) — H = “GC-rich (high)”, L = “normal (low)”. Symbols: the bases \(\{A, C, G, T\}\).
\[
\boldsymbol{\pi}=\begin{bmatrix} 0.5 \\ 0.5 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
A=\begin{array}{cc}
& \begin{array}{cc} H & L \end{array}\\
\begin{array}{c} H\\ L \end{array} &
\left[\begin{array}{cc} 0.8 & 0.2 \\ 0.3 & 0.7 \end{array}\right]
\end{array}
\]
\[
B=\begin{array}{c}
\begin{array}{cccc} \ A\  & \ C\  & \ G\  & \ T\  \end{array}\\
\begin{array}{c} \end{array}
\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
0.1 & 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.1 \\
0.3 & 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.3
\end{array}\right]
\end{array}
\begin{array}{c} H\\ L \end{array}
\]
Read \(B\): state \(H\) emits C or G with prob 0.4 each (GC-rich!); state \(L\) spreads probability more evenly.


This is the example we will compute with by hand, and again in Fitting HMMs in R, so introduce it carefully. Pi says we start in either state with equal chance. A says both states are sticky — H stays H with probability 0.8, L stays L with 0.7 — which encodes that islands and background come in runs. B is the new object: the high state loves C and G, the low state is roughly uniform. Sanity check every row sums to one.

Good practice checklist
Independent of the specific method, a trustworthy spectral model:

preprocesses consistently, with all choices fixed on training data only;
keeps a truly independent test set, touched once, at the end;
tunes complexity (components, variables) by cross-validation, respecting sample structure (replicates, batches);
reports error in interpretable units (RMSEP) with bias and a class-appropriate metric;
checks for outliers and applicability (\(T^2\), \(Q\)) rather than blindly predicting;
is honest about transfer: does it still work on a new instrument, day, or population?

If a result cannot survive this checklist, it is not yet a result.

[REVIEW] This slide is the practical takeaway and could stand as the unit’s summary of judgement. Confirm it matches the course’s stance elsewhere. It intentionally contains no numbers to invent.

The full model, assembled
// gene_marker.stan
data {
  int&lt;lower=0&gt; N;                 // cells assayed
  int&lt;lower=0&gt; y;                 // cells expressing the marker
}
parameters {
  real&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; theta;   // fraction expressing
}
model {
  theta ~ beta(2, 2);             // prior
  y ~ binomial(N, theta);         // likelihood
}
That is a complete, runnable Bayesian model. Save it as gene_marker.stan. Three blocks, seven lines of logic — this is the whole thing.

Show the assembled file so the pieces cohere into one artifact. Re-read it top to bottom in plain English: “we know N and y; we want theta; theta is a priori around a half; the data are Binomial.” Point out how compact a full Bayesian model is once the language is familiar — and that this exact skeleton generalises: swap the likelihood line for a Poisson or negative binomial (from the distributions block) and you have a count model instead. Reassure that this file is genuinely complete and would run as-is.

Each cell is a point in gene space
Stack the normalized data as a matrix: rows = cells, columns = genes.
\[
X = \begin{pmatrix}
x_{11} & x_{12} & \cdots & x_{1g} \\
x_{21} & x_{22} & \cdots & x_{2g} \\
\vdots & & \ddots & \vdots \\
x_{n1} & x_{n2} & \cdots & x_{ng}
\end{pmatrix}
\qquad
\begin{array}{l}
n \approx \text{thousands of cells} \\
g \approx \text{2000 genes}
\end{array}
\]

Row \(i\) is one cell: a point in a \(g\)-dimensional space (one axis per gene).
“Similar cells” = points whose rows are close, e.g. small Euclidean distance \(\lVert \mathbf{x}_i - \mathbf{x}_j \rVert\).

We cannot plot a 2000-dimensional space. We need to compress it to 2D without destroying the neighbourhood structure. That is the whole problem.


Make the matrix concrete and connect to the sample × feature matrices they already know from the statistics course — here samples are cells and features are (highly variable) genes. Emphasise that after preprocessing we usually keep only the ~2000 highly variable genes, not all 20000, because most genes are uninformative noise. Restate the geometric definition of similarity as small distance, which they met in vector geometry. Then state the crux: 2000 dimensions is unplottable and also noisy, so we must reduce — and the reduction must protect who-is-near-whom.

